 relenting  in a rare move the parents of the  michigan teen accused of fatally  shooting four students at his high  school are facing four counts each of  involuntary manslaughter prosecutors say  james and jennifer crumbly purchased a  gun and gave it to their 15-year-old son  ethan ignoring the school's warnings  about his potential for violence state  authorities say the parents are now  missing u.s marshals and the fbi are  involved in the search ethan crumbly has  been charged as an adult with two dozen  crimes  the taliban is banning the forced  marriage of women in afghanistan its  chief issued the decree saying men and  women should be treated equal the move  addresses one of the demands set out by  the international community to recognize  the taliban as afghanistan's government  and to restore aid poverty is surging in  the country since the taliban takeover  in august since then thousands of girls  have been banned from attending school  and most women haven't been allowed to  return to work  canada's skyrocketing house prices ahead  which buyers are fueling the surge  it's been a tough couple of years for  the economy but there is one sector in  this country that remains hot real  estate home sales in vancouver are up  nearly 12  over last year the benchmark home price  hit 1.2 million dollars in toronto the  average home price is now above 1.1  million up almost 22 percent year over  year those sky-high prices are being  bolstered by domestic investors  canadians carrying two or more mortgages  and gaviola explains  gravity-defying home prices have become  a feature of the covet era reflecting a  rush of people getting into the market  with low mortgage rates many seeking  larger living quarters more green space  a change of scenery but there's another  big driver canadian investors not buying  a place to live but a vehicle for profit  real estate continues to be something  that attracts people ramona king helps  run a real estate search company she  says a lot of homeowners in vc's lower  mainland have taken advantage of higher  valuations in their primary residence to  buy another home and they purchased a  vacation property there that they also  know they can rent out so they can get  an income home purchases by domestic  investors soared during the pandemic  outpacing first-time home buyers and  repeat buyers according to the bank of  canada  terranet data shows that purchases by  people who own more than one property  climb two representing more than 25  percent of all transactions in the first  half of this year  analysis by equifax shows a bump in the  number of households carrying four  mortgages or more if you look at some of  the other provinces like uh alberta bc  there's a little bit of growth um but  you know not to the same degree that we  see in ontario and quebec and actually  manitoba has seen quite a lot as well in  a recent report the central bank says  the sudden influx of investors likely  drove up home prices and exposes the  market to a higher chance of a  correction  interest rates could be a concern next  year um you know on some like  non-mortgage debt but it really depends  you know i think consumers probably have  more buffer than we kind of have ever  seen before with interest rate hikes  looming next year and a big question  mark about how long the housing market  can stay this hot the prominence of  investor capital is making it harder for  first-time homebuyers to find a home ann  gaviola global news toronto  next the plan to protect canada's  susceptible species from cobot 19.  canadian zoos are eagerly awaiting a  package delivery but it has nothing to  do with the holidays a u.s  pharmaceutical company is donating 900  doses of a coveted vaccine made for  animals it's a product not on the market  yet but as mike armstrong explains it's  been approved for experimental use by  regulators on both sides of the border  there is a challenge in caring for  animals it's not just that they don't  tell you they're sick they actually hide  it it's instinctual in the wild they  can't look sick in case there are  predators around they do a really good  job of looking strong and great and then  can be susceptible to really rapid  decline well the toronto zoo is one of  six in canada about to get a new tool to  keep its animals from getting sick a  pharmaceutical company based in new  jersey has developed a vaccine  specifically for animals it started as a  product for pets cats and dogs but that  hasn't been necessary there have however  been cases in zoo animals the canadian  food inspection agency has approved it  for emergency use and the company says  it should ship in the coming days  south of the border the u.s department  of agriculture has also approved its use  and vaccinations are already underway  including at one of the biggest in the  country the smithsonian's national zoo  in washington d.c so far 76 animals have  received at least one dose the zoo's  chief veterinarian is very happy his  animals are now better protected in  september they did have an outbreak nine  lions and tigers were coughing and  sneezing tests confirmed it was coveted  19. we were hoping oh in a few days  they're going to be better because  that's what they do and that's not what  they all did three animals were severely  sick one we actually thought we talked  about euthanasia at one point due to  renal failure now there have been deaths  at some zoos from the coronavirus last  month the lincoln zoo in nebraska lost  three rare snow leopards  according to the association that  represents canadian zoos its members are  eager to get the vaccine but to this  point there have been no cases  we're very fortunate in canada thus far  knock on wood  but i know that that they're all  chomping at the bit  the washington zoo hopes to complete its  vaccination program by the end of  january for the most part it hasn't been  as hard as one might expect a lot of the  animals have been trained to accept  needles in return for treats some of  them get frozen juice treats  it might you know in the case of our  giant pandas they get honey water  now the washington zoo is also going to  be holding a vaccination session next  week for humans something that compared  to animals has so far been a bigger  challenge  mike armstrong global news washington  and that is global national for this  friday night i'm colleen christie  tonight's york canada is central park in  the city of spruce grove alberta all lit  up for the holidays we love seeing your  canada please email your holiday themed  photos to viewers at globalnational.com  thanks for watching robin gill will be  at the anchor desk tomorrow have a great  night
 

